<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Android Network Security Config for rustdar.

  Referenced by android:networkSecurityConfig on <application> in
  AndroidManifest.xml, and compiled into resources.arsc by aapt2 as part of the
  Gradle build.

  This file is why that Gradle build exists. cargo-apk, which built the APK
  before, could not deliver it:

    * Its AndroidManifest generator has a fixed attribute set on <application>
      (debuggable, theme, extractNativeLibs, usesCleartextTraffic, label, icon,
      hasCode, meta-data). `networkSecurityConfig` is not in it, and the metadata
      table in Cargo.toml has no passthrough for arbitrary attributes.
    * The attribute's value is a *resource reference*, so the file has to be
      compiled by aapt2 into `resources.arsc` and given a resource ID. The
      `classes.dex` injection that build-android.sh used to do worked only
      because a DEX at the APK root needs no resource table entry; an `res/xml/`
      file does. Adding it to the ZIP would have achieved nothing.

  ===========================================================================
  Why the base config permits cleartext while every rustdar domain forbids it

  rustls-platform-verifier issue #221: on Android, certificates whose revocation
  data is published over plain HTTP fail to verify when the app forbids cleartext
  traffic. Android's TrustManager fetches the CRL itself, and that fetch is
  subject to the app's own network security policy; when it is blocked the
  verifier cannot prove the certificate is unrevoked and rejects it. The cause is
  the blocked fetch, not a hard-fail revocation policy.

  api.weather.gov is exactly that shape. Verified against the live endpoint:

      issuer  = C=US, O=Let's Encrypt, CN=R13
      AIA     = CA Issuers - URI:http://r13.i.lencr.org/   (no OCSP URI at all)
      CRL DP  = URI:http://r13.c.lencr.org/32.crl          (cleartext)

  With no OCSP responder to fall back on, that cleartext CRL is the only
  revocation source, so blocking cleartext app-wide breaks HTTPS to weather.gov.

  Hence: `cleartextTrafficPermitted="true"` in base-config, so the platform's own
  revocation fetches succeed, and `false` per-domain for every host rustdar
  actually talks to, so none of rustdar's own traffic can travel in the clear.
  Application code is not trusted to get this right on its own (every client is
  additionally built with `.https_only(true)`, see rustdar_radar::tls::client),
  but this file is what makes it the OS's decision rather than ours.

  NB: an XML comment may not contain a double hyphen, and aapt2 parses this file
  for real. Keep prose here free of them or the resource build fails.

  includeSubdomains is set where a host family is used (*.noaa.gov, *.weather.gov)
  and omitted where the exact host is fixed.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <!-- Permits the platform's own cleartext CRL/AIA fetches. Does NOT permit
         rustdar's traffic: every domain below overrides it back to false. -->
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <!-- System store only, and explicitly no bundled roots: trust must
                 stay revocable by the OS and updatable without a new binary.
                 `user` is deliberately absent from the *default*; add it in a
                 debug-only overlay if you need to MITM for debugging. -->
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>

    <!-- NWS / NOAA: alerts, zones, METAR, SPC, HRRR, Level III over TGFTP. -->
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">weather.gov</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">noaa.gov</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">aviationweather.gov</domain>
    </domain-config>

    <!-- NEXRAD Level II archive and realtime chunks, via nexrad-data. -->
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">s3.amazonaws.com</domain>
    </domain-config>

    <!-- Basemap and label tiles (walkers -> CartoDB via Fastly). -->
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">global.ssl.fastly.net</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">openstreetmap.org</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
